"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[1],{7001:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2016-04-08T16:44:00","modified":"2016-04-08T16:45:00","id":1026,"title":"Debug your Gulp/Grunt builds with VS Code","article":"<p>When running gulp tasks I sometimes have issues with it not building correctly, usually the error output to the console is good enough to work out what is going on, but occasionally it would be helpful if&nbsp;we could see more of the process as it failed.</p>\\n\\n<p>There are several options available; First we can run gulp using the <a href=\\"https://nodejs.org/api/debugger.html\\">built-in&nbsp;node debugger</a> from the command, however that has limitations as it can be painful to run through&nbsp;line by line. Another option is a dev tools inspector such as <a href=\\"https://github.com/node-inspector/node-inspector\\" target=\\"_blank\\">Node Inspector</a> or <a href=\\"http://s-a.github.io/iron-node/\\">Iron Node</a>&nbsp;which allows you to run and inspect the code in chromium dev tools, which ships with Google Chrome. These are great to use, but as of time of writing both caused me issues with gulp-sass so I decided to look at the third option, the debugger within <a href=\\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging\\">VS Code</a> and use it with gulp.</p>\\n\\n<p>VS Code has alot of flexibility in what you can make it run when simply pressing F5, we just have to configure what it does.</p>\\n\\n<p>To start have your project open in VS Code</p>\\n\\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\\n\\n<ul>\\n\\t<li><a href=\\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/editor/debugging\\">VS Code - Debugging</a></li>\\n\\t<li><a href=\\"http://s-a.github.io/iron-node/\\">Iron Node</a></li>\\n\\t<li><a href=\\"https://nodejs.org/api/debugger.html\\">Node Built-in Debugger</a></li>\\n</ul>\\n","showInResults":false,"tags":["nodejs","javascript","vs code"]}')}}]);